<article class="env-lemma" id="0EJ4">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EJ4">Lemma <span data-tag="0EJ4">52.19.2</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0EHC" data-tag="0EHC">52.16.1</a> let $(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ be an object of $\textit{Coh}(U, I\mathcal{O}_ U)$. Let $a, b$ be integers. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ is annihilated by a power of $I$, then $(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ satisfies the $(a, b)$-inequalities for any $a, b$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ satisfies the $(a + 1, b)$-inequalities, then $(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ satisfies the strict $(a, b)$-inequalities. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> If $\text{cd}(A, I) \leq d$ and $A$ has a dualizing complex, then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(3)"><p>
$(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ satisfies the $(s, s + d)$-inequalities if and only if for all $y \in U \cap Y$ the tuple $\mathcal{O}_{X, y}^\wedge , I\mathcal{O}_{X, y}^\wedge , \{ \mathfrak m_ y^\wedge \} , \mathcal{F}_ y^\wedge , s - \delta ^ Y_ Z(y), d$ is as in Situation <a href="/tag/0EFU" data-tag="0EFU">52.10.1</a>. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(4)"><p>
If $(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ satisfies the strict $(s, s + d)$-inequalities, then $(\mathcal{F}_ n)$ satisfies the $(s, s + d)$-inequalities. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Immediate except for part (4) which is a consequence of Lemma <a href="/tag/0EFW" data-tag="0EFW">52.10.5</a> and the translation in (3). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>